    export const skill = "---\nname: js-interactivity\ndescription: Guidance for writing small, defensive vanilla JavaScript that implements a fixed list of interactions against real HTML, without over-building or breaking accessibility.\n---\n\n# JS Interactivity\n\nYou're implementing a specific, finite list of interactions against HTML\nthat already exists - this skill is about doing that defensively and\naccessibly, not about which framework patterns to use (there isn't one;\nthis is vanilla JS against the real DOM).\n\n## Query defensively, every time\n\nThe HTML was generated by a separate step and, occasionally, won't contain\nexactly what's expected. Before attaching behavior to an element:\n- Check a single element exists (`if (!el) return;`) before calling methods\n  on it.\n- Check a collection is non-empty (`if (!items.length) return;`) before\n  looping or attaching per-item listeners.\n- Never assume a selector match - a script that throws on a missing element\n  breaks every interaction defined after it in the same file, not just the\n  one that was missing its target.\n\n## Delegate instead of attaching per-item\n\nFor any repeated element (a list of cards, a set of accordion triggers), a\nsingle listener on the shared parent with `event.target.closest(...)` to\nidentify which item was interacted with is both more efficient and more\ncorrect than one listener per item - it also automatically covers items\nadded to the DOM later, which a per-item loop attached once at load time\ndoes not.\n\n## Keep interactive JS accessible, not just clickable\n\nA JS-driven toggle (mobile menu, accordion, dropdown, modal) has\naccessibility obligations beyond making the click work:\n- Toggle `aria-expanded` on the trigger element to match its actual state -\n  this is the one place JS and HTML have to stay in sync, since the HTML\n  stage sets the initial value but only the JS can update it at runtime.\n- Support closing on `Escape` for anything that opens an overlay (modal,\n  dropdown, mobile menu), and support opening/activating on `Enter`/`Space`\n  for any custom interactive element that isn't a native `<button>`.\n- Moving focus matters for modals/menus: when one opens, move focus into it\n  (or its first focusable element); when it closes, return focus to the\n  trigger that opened it, so keyboard users aren't left focused on a\n  now-hidden element.\n- Don't rely on `:hover` alone to reveal functionality - anything a mouse\n  user can do by hovering needs a keyboard/tap equivalent.\n\n## Build only what's on the interaction list\n\nEvery interaction implemented should trace back to a specific entry in the\narchitecture's interaction list - not a plausible-sounding addition. A\ncarousel, theme toggle, or form-validation script that wasn't asked for is\nextra surface area for bugs, extra weight, and a behavior the design/content\nstages never accounted for. An empty interaction list means a script that\ndoes nothing (or doesn't exist) is the correct output, not a missed\nopportunity.\n\n## Side effects stay contained\n\n- Wrap all DOM-touching setup in `DOMContentLoaded` so nothing runs before\n  the elements it needs exist.\n- Prefer small named functions over one large script body - each function\n  should correspond to one interaction, making it obvious which code\n  implements which listed behavior.\n- Clean up what you create: a `setInterval`/`setTimeout` tied to an element\n  that can be removed or hidden should be cleared, not left running against\n  a detached node.\n- Don't create global variables beyond what a genuinely shared piece of\n  state needs; scope everything else inside the function that uses it.\n\n## Don't restructure what the HTML/CSS stages already decided\n\nJS should attach behavior to the existing DOM, not compensate for it -\ninserting new wrapper elements, inline styles, or `className` overwrites to\nfix a layout issue means the layout should have been fixed in the HTML/CSS\nstages instead. The one legitimate exception is DOM creation an interaction\nexplicitly requires (a toast that's created and removed on submit, options\nappended to a `<select>` from data) - creating structure the interaction\nitself calls for is expected; patching structure the interaction doesn't\nneed is not.\n";
  